lessons [{":activity/expected-seconds": 720.0, ":activity/first-step": {":db/id": 17592186184906}, ":activity/id": "8038dbd6-9c0f-5fe5-be97-e9a0082ee6ea", ":activity/scope": {":db/id": 17592186050321}, ":activity/steps": [{":db/id": 17592186184906, ":step/content": {":db/id": 17592186185366, ":tutorial/content": "How can we calculate the integral of a sum of functions, like $\displaystyle \int (x+x^3)\,\textrm d x?$ 

According to the **sum rule**, the integral of the sum of functions is equal to the sum of the integrals of each function:

$$
\int (f(x) \pm g(x))\,\textrm d x = \int f(x) \,\textrm d x \pm \int g(x) \,\textrm d x.
$$

In our case, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}\int (x+{x}^{3})\,\text{d}x & =\int x\,\text{d}x+\int {x}^{3}\,\text{d}x \\  & =(\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{c}_{1})+(\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+{c}_{2}) \\  & =\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2}) \\  & =\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+C,\end{aligned}
$$

where we renamed $c_1+c_2=C.$ (The sum of two arbitrary constants is itself an arbitrary constant.)", ":tutorial/id": "207bbd12-158a-58be-b000-82cace6dcdde", ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 701, ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"}, ":step/id": "61a29b78-51b2-509a-bb12-5c2009c1ee92", ":step/math-academy-id": 1351, ":step/next": {":db/id": 17592186184907}}, {":db/id": 17592186184907, ":step/content": {":db/id": 17592186184153, ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {":db/id": 17592186184057, ":question/id": "e4a94ca8-82ee-5776-a46d-b735d4ab60fb", ":question/math-academy-id": "e-371", ":question/problem": "Find the integral of $x^2 + 1.$", ":question/worked-solution": "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:

